WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.962
Take this marked hexagon and surround it
with matching copies.

2
00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:07.220
One complete ring fits. Then another, then
a third.

3
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.949
How long can a shape keep growing before
the next ring becomes impossible?

4
00:00:11.949 --> 00:00:14.760
That is the question behind a Heesch
number.

5
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.474
A ring is called a corona. Here, complete
means that the new cluster fully

6
00:00:20.474 --> 00:00:24.949
encloses the old one, with no overlaps,
holes, or point pinches.

7
00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:27.956
The union must be a solid disk-like region.

8
00:00:27.956 --> 00:00:31.452
These strict rules matter when checking a
picture.

9
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.126
A shape that tiles the entire plane can
keep growing indefinitely.

10
00:00:36.126 --> 00:00:39.814
A non-tiling shape may still surround
itself several times.

11
00:00:39.814 --> 00:00:44.690
The challenge is to distinguish an
impressive patch from the largest number of

12
00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:47.816
rings that any permitted construction can
achieve.

13
00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:52.239
This study asks a bounded version of that
question.

14
00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:55.379
Fix one of thirty-four small base shapes.

15
00:00:55.379 --> 00:01:00.050
Give each boundary edge one centred bump,
nick, or flat mark.

16
00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:05.641
Keep rotated, reflected and translated
copies on the prescribed square or

17
00:01:05.641 --> 00:01:06.789
hexagonal grid.

18
00:01:07.331 --> 00:01:11.540
A bump must fit a nick, while a flat edge
meets another flat edge.

19
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:16.131
Two bumps collide, and a nick facing a flat
edge leaves a forbidden gap.

20
00:01:16.131 --> 00:01:20.531
These matching rules turn an ordinary shape
into a jigsaw-like piece.

21
00:01:21.065 --> 00:01:25.796
Our single-hexagon witness has three bumps,
two nicks, and one flat edge.

22
00:01:25.796 --> 00:01:30.073
Bumps outnumber nicks by one. Such a
marking is called unbalanced.

23
00:01:30.073 --> 00:01:35.129
We search over every unbalanced marking,
not only this particular arrangement.

24
00:01:35.665 --> 00:01:40.532
When two pieces meet correctly, an internal
bump and nick cancel in the count.

25
00:01:40.532 --> 00:01:43.590
Each copy still contributes one net surplus
bump.

26
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:48.207
In a finite patch, that uncancelled surplus
has to be carried by the outer

27
00:01:48.207 --> 00:01:48.769
boundary.

28
00:01:49.298 --> 00:01:53.998
For larger and larger patches, the number
of pieces grows like area, while the

29
00:01:53.998 --> 00:01:56.348
boundary capacity grows like perimeter.

30
00:01:56.348 --> 00:01:58.397
Eventually the surplus cannot fit.

31
00:01:58.397 --> 00:02:02.736
The written geometric argument makes this
precise for the curved pieces,

32
00:02:02.736 --> 00:02:06.050
showing that an unbalanced piece cannot
tile the plane.

33
00:02:06.595 --> 00:02:11.322
Now build the actual three-ring witness
from its retained placements.

34
00:02:11.322 --> 00:02:15.775
Start with one central piece, then add six,
twelve, and eighteen.

35
00:02:15.775 --> 00:02:18.516
That gives thirty-seven pieces in total.

36
00:02:18.516 --> 00:02:23.654
Every displayed orientation comes from the
checked witness, not an invented

37
00:02:23.654 --> 00:02:24.955
decorative pattern.

38
00:02:25.495 --> 00:02:29.663
For a single hexagon, a hypothetical fourth
ring would contain sixty-one

39
00:02:29.663 --> 00:02:33.077
hexagons altogether. Its outside has only
fifty-four edges.

40
00:02:33.077 --> 00:02:37.302
Each unbalanced piece contributes at least
one surplus mark, so the patch

41
00:02:37.302 --> 00:02:39.559
would need room for at least sixty-one.

42
00:02:40.095 --> 00:02:43.045
Sixty-one cannot fit into fifty-four slots.

43
00:02:43.045 --> 00:02:47.365
That is the classical counting obstruction
credited to Epstein.

44
00:02:47.365 --> 00:02:50.795
Three rings are attained, and four are
impossible.

45
00:02:50.795 --> 00:02:56.007
Larger base pieces have more complicated
boundaries, so this simple count no

46
00:02:56.007 --> 00:02:58.407
longer settles their best markings.

47
00:02:58.927 --> 00:03:01.848
Consider a straight row of four hexagons.

48
00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:07.121
It has eighteen outer edges. The retained
optimal witness uses nine bumps,

49
00:03:07.121 --> 00:03:12.251
eight nicks, and one flat edge. Around the
boundary they form a block of

50
00:03:12.251 --> 00:03:14.887
bumps, a flat, then a block of nicks.

51
00:03:15.427 --> 00:03:18.727
Place the exact central row, then its first
ring.

52
00:03:18.727 --> 00:03:23.038
The cluster now contains twenty-four
underlying hexagonal cells.

53
00:03:23.038 --> 00:03:26.540
Add the second ring and there are
sixty-eight cells.

54
00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.659
Each whole marked row counts as one piece,
so cells and pieces are different

55
00:03:31.659 --> 00:03:32.131
counts.

56
00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:35.393
Continue with the third, fourth and fifth
rings.

57
00:03:35.393 --> 00:03:39.834
The total cell counts become one hundred
and sixty, three hundred and sixteen,

58
00:03:39.834 --> 00:03:44.104
and five hundred and twenty-four. Dividing
the last count by four gives one

59
00:03:44.104 --> 00:03:45.812
hundred and thirty-one pieces.

60
00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:50.489
This picture proves that this marking
reaches at least five rings.

61
00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:54.555
It does not by itself show that another
marking cannot reach six.

62
00:03:54.555 --> 00:03:59.434
The new question is a maximum over all
unbalanced choices of the eighteen edge

63
00:03:59.434 --> 00:03:59.872
labels.

64
00:04:00.391 --> 00:04:04.519
Each edge has three labels, so eighteen
edges give three to the eighteenth

65
00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:08.703
possibilities: more than three hundred and
eighty-seven million assignments

66
00:04:08.703 --> 00:04:10.656
before restrictions and symmetries.

67
00:04:10.656 --> 00:04:15.007
Checking each marking separately would
repeat much of the same geometric work.

68
00:04:15.524 --> 00:04:20.377
Instead, make both the marking and the
placements part of one yes-or-no

69
00:04:20.377 --> 00:04:23.794
puzzle. A switch says whether a placement
is used.

70
00:04:23.794 --> 00:04:26.391
Other switches choose its edge labels.

71
00:04:26.391 --> 00:04:31.654
Rules forbid overlap, enforce matching, and
require the requested surrounding

72
00:04:31.654 --> 00:04:32.132
layers.

73
00:04:32.658 --> 00:04:36.297
This is a satisfiability problem, often
shortened to SAT.

74
00:04:36.297 --> 00:04:39.872
All the switches must obey all the rules
simultaneously.

75
00:04:39.872 --> 00:04:43.958
One additional counting rule requires at
least one surplus bump.

76
00:04:43.958 --> 00:04:48.810
Swapping bumps and nicks covers the
opposite imbalance in the lattice model.

77
00:04:49.358 --> 00:04:51.515
The window is finite for a reason.

78
00:04:51.515 --> 00:04:56.338
A piece in the next ring must touch the
preceding cluster, so it cannot jump

79
00:04:56.338 --> 00:05:00.908
arbitrarily far away. The paper proves a
distance bound for every layer.

80
00:05:00.908 --> 00:05:05.414
The search window contains every possible
patch at the requested depth.

81
00:05:05.958 --> 00:05:09.364
A yes answer comes with actual placements
and marks.

82
00:05:09.364 --> 00:05:14.409
A separate short program reconstructs the
patch, checks matching and overlap,

83
00:05:14.409 --> 00:05:19.257
and checks that each growing union is a
disk enclosing the previous union.

84
00:05:19.257 --> 00:05:22.206
The saved geometry is the witness we
animate.

85
00:05:22.724 --> 00:05:25.162
A no answer needs a different receipt.

86
00:05:25.162 --> 00:05:29.845
The solver writes a sequence of logical
proof steps, called a DRAT proof.

87
00:05:29.845 --> 00:05:34.656
A separate checker verifies that those
steps establish inconsistency of the

88
00:05:34.656 --> 00:05:39.403
encoded puzzle. The written encoding
argument connects that puzzle back to

89
00:05:39.403 --> 00:05:39.788
rings.

90
00:05:40.321 --> 00:05:45.165
For the straight four-row, five rings have
a witness and six are excluded for

91
00:05:45.165 --> 00:05:49.757
every unbalanced marking. The same maximum
of five holds for the straight

92
00:05:49.757 --> 00:05:54.664
five-row. These are exact maxima in the
stated lattice model, not upper bounds

93
00:05:54.664 --> 00:05:56.929
for arbitrary off-grid arrangements.

94
00:05:57.454 --> 00:06:02.796
The full ledger covers thirty-four bases:
twelve shapes made from at most four

95
00:06:02.796 --> 00:06:07.590
hexagons, twenty-one made from at most five
squares, plus the straight

96
00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:12.932
five-hexagon row. Every entry has a checked
witness and a verified upper-bound

97
00:06:12.932 --> 00:06:13.822
proof record.

98
00:06:14.354 --> 00:06:19.509
For straight hexagon rows of lengths one
through five, the maxima are three,

99
00:06:19.509 --> 00:06:24.665
four, four, five, and five. Among the
twelve bases of at most four hexagons,

100
00:06:24.665 --> 00:06:27.378
only the straight four-row reaches five.

101
00:06:27.378 --> 00:06:30.362
The non-straight shapes reach at most
three.

102
00:06:30.888 --> 00:06:36.145
For the square-cell bases, the maximum is
three, achieved by the straight rows

103
00:06:36.145 --> 00:06:40.053
of four and five squares. The other values
are one or two.

104
00:06:40.053 --> 00:06:45.243
Six non-row optimal witnesses have
imbalance two or three, so the row pattern

105
00:06:45.243 --> 00:06:47.400
does not describe every optimum.

106
00:06:47.921 --> 00:06:51.263
The checks also try deliberately broken
witnesses.

107
00:06:51.263 --> 00:06:54.405
One hundred and seventy mutations are
rejected.

108
00:06:54.405 --> 00:06:59.552
A second encoder agrees on both tested
bounds for thirty-two bases, excluding

109
00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:04.298
the two largest row cases. These
cross-checks support the model without

110
00:07:04.298 --> 00:07:07.841
pretending every implementation is formally
verified.

111
00:07:08.384 --> 00:07:11.453
Now separate this result from the planar
record.

112
00:07:11.453 --> 00:07:16.249
Mann already constructed five-ring
hexapillars, and the Heesch Number Atlas

113
00:07:16.249 --> 00:07:20.213
lists five for particular markings of the
four- and five-rows.

114
00:07:20.213 --> 00:07:24.752
This package does not announce a new
five-ring example or a new record.

115
00:07:25.284 --> 00:07:29.847
Its narrower contribution is the maximum
over all unbalanced single-slot

116
00:07:29.847 --> 00:07:33.079
markings, with replayable lower and upper
evidence.

117
00:07:33.079 --> 00:07:37.705
Possible overlap with Mann and Thomas's
smaller tables remains unresolved

118
00:07:37.705 --> 00:07:40.240
because their full text was unavailable.

119
00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.204
Novelty is therefore qualified.

120
00:07:42.751 --> 00:07:47.824
Bojan Bashich's published six-ring
construction is a separate existing result.

121
00:07:47.824 --> 00:07:52.052
Here is its retained geometry: one hundred
and sixty-nine copies.

122
00:07:52.052 --> 00:07:57.060
The package audits that published patch
exactly, including the strict no-hole

123
00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:02.068
and no-pinch requirements. It confirms the
construction rather than extending

124
00:08:02.068 --> 00:08:02.263
it.

125
00:08:02.784 --> 00:08:05.133
Watch the six layers arrive in order.

126
00:08:05.133 --> 00:08:09.576
They contain six, ten, thirteen,
thirty-two, fifty-two, and fifty-five

127
00:08:09.576 --> 00:08:09.956
pieces

128
00:08:09.956 --> 00:08:14.526
around the centre. At each stage, the
boundary was checked as one simple

129
00:08:14.526 --> 00:08:19.032
closed cycle, so a pinched drawing cannot
quietly pass as a valid disk.

130
00:08:19.551 --> 00:08:24.701
The upper-bound proof files for the largest
puzzles were checked in completed

131
00:08:24.701 --> 00:08:29.583
runs. The retained package stores their
hashes, commands and verification

132
00:08:29.583 --> 00:08:32.659
logs; it does not ship every large proof
file.

133
00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:37.407
No claim is made that video production
replayed those expensive proofs.

134
00:08:37.948 --> 00:08:40.450
Remember the boundary of the result.

135
00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:43.717
One centred mark per edge. Unbalanced
markings.

136
00:08:43.717 --> 00:08:47.124
Strict coronas. Copies on the prescribed
lattice.

137
00:08:47.124 --> 00:08:52.407
More mark slots, balanced non-tilers, or
copies sliding off that lattice are

138
00:08:52.407 --> 00:08:57.412
different questions. No global off-grid
upper bound is established here.

139
00:08:57.948 --> 00:09:02.735
The proof pattern has two halves. A
concrete picture shows what can be built.

140
00:09:02.735 --> 00:09:07.212
A checked impossibility proof rules out the
next layer for every allowed

141
00:09:07.212 --> 00:09:12.000
marking. Combining them turns a successful
construction into an exact maximum

142
00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.052
within a clearly specified model.

143
00:09:14.581 --> 00:09:18.657
Nothing here establishes a planar Heesch
number of seven.

144
00:09:18.657 --> 00:09:23.805
Correctness confidence is high inside the
lattice model; novelty is more

145
00:09:23.805 --> 00:09:29.025
limited. The paper, retained witnesses and
editable scenes accompany this

146
00:09:29.025 --> 00:09:31.885
video. Narration uses a synthetic voice.
